{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "rp-08-changing-ratios",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Changing Ratios after Adding, Removing, or Transferring",
    "description": "Model changing ratios with one equation before and one equation after the change.",
    "teachingApproach": "Begin with two numbers whose ratio changes after the same addition. Then vary one side, transfers, ages, and mixtures. Keep the original amounts as ratio multiples and apply the change to the actual expressions, never to the ratio terms alone.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 13, Ratio and Proportion. Approved scope: Teach changing-ratio equations. Start with adding or subtracting the same number from both terms, then add/remove people from one group, transfer units from one group to another, and handle age ratios after a fixed number of years. Include mixture questions where only water or milk is added, and two-condition problems that determine both original quantities. Emphasize that ratio terms are not actual values until multiplied by a common scale."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Ratios change when quantities change—but the change must be applied to the actual quantities, not to the ratio digits.\n\nWe’ll model additions, removals, transfers, age shifts, and one-component mixture changes with one clean equation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Two numbers are in ratio 3:5, so call them 3k and 5k.\n\nIf 4 is added to both and the ratio becomes 5:7, write:\n(3k+4)/(5k+4)=5/7.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Cross-multiply:\n21k+28 = 25k+20\n4k=8 -> k=2.\n\nThe original numbers were 6 and 10.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
